air among D, E, F. So any sunny line covering two of them is impossible. Therefore we must rely on covering at least one of D, E, F by a sunny line that also covers a point from column n-2 (or maybe from column n-3 if we had vertical lines there, but those are already used). So each sunny line can cover at most one of D, E, F, plus possibly a point from column n-2. Since we have two sunny lines, we can cover at most two of D, E, F. The third remains uncovered. So we cannot cover all three points with only two sunny lines if L0 is vertical x=n-2. So vertical x=n-2 fails.\n\nCase 2: L0 = horizontal y=1. Then L0 covers points A=(n-2,1), D=(n-1,1), F=(n,1). So three points covered. Remaining points: B=(n-2,2), C=(n-2,3), E=(n-1,2). Need to cover these three points with two sunny lines. Let's see if possible.\n\nWe need to cover B, C, E. B and C are in same column n-2, C is at y=3, B at y=2. E is column n-1, y=2.\n\nWe have two sunny lines. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. So we cannot have a sunny line covering both B and C (same column). So B and C must be covered by different sunny lines. Then E must be covered by one of those sunny lines (or a third line, but we only have two). So one of the sunny lines must cover both E and either B or C. Let's examine possibilities:\n\n- Sunny line covering B (n-2,2) and E (n-1,2): slope = (2-2)/(n-1-(n-2)) = 0, horizontal, not allowed.\n\n- Sunny line covering C (n-2,3) and E (n-1,2): slope = (2-3)/(n-1-(n-2)) = -1, not allowed.\n\nThus no sunny line can cover E together with B or C. So E must be covered by a sunny line that also covers a point from column n-2 that is not B or C? But there are only points in column n-2: A (already covered by L0), B, C. So we could have a sunny line covering E and A (n-2,1). Let's compute slope between A (n-2,1) and E (n-1,2): slope = (2-1)/(n-1-(n-2)) = 1, which is sunny. So a sunny line L1 could pass through A and E. However, A is already covered by L0, but that's okay. The