1 vertical lines + 1 sunny line. For k=2, maybe we can use n-2 vertical lines + 2 sunny lines. But we need to check if that can cover all points.\n\nIf we use vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-2 (that's n-2 vertical lines). Then we have columns n-1 and n left. Column n-1 has points (n-1,1) and (n-1,2). Column n has point (n,1). So we need to cover these three points with 2 sunny lines. Could we do that? Possibly: one sunny line could pass through (n-1,1) and (n,1) (i.e., horizontal line y=1). But horizontal line is non-sunny. So not allowed for sunny lines. So we need to use sunny lines for these points. Could we have a sunny line covering (n-1,1) and (n-1,2)? That would be vertical line x=n-1, which is non-sunny. So not allowed. Could we have a sunny line covering (n-1,1) and (n,1)? That's horizontal line y=1, non-sunny. So not allowed. Could we have a sunny line covering (n-1,2) and (n,1)? That line has slope (1-2)/(n-(n-1)) = -1/1 = -1, so slope -1, which is non-sunny. So not allowed. So any line connecting any two of these three points is either vertical, horizontal, or slope -1, all non-sunny. So a sunny line can cover at most one of these three points. So we need at least three lines to cover them, but we only have 2 sunny lines left (since we used n-2 vertical lines). So we cannot cover all points with exactly 2 sunny lines if we use vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-2. Could we use a different arrangement of non-sunny lines? Perhaps we could use some horizontal lines or diagonal lines to cover more points, reducing the need for vertical lines. But we need to cover all points with n lines total. Let's explore possibilities.\n\nWe need to cover all points in the triangular region. The total number of points is n(n+1)/2. Each line can cover at most some number of points. Non-sunny lines can cover many points: vertical line x=i covers n+1-i points; horizontal line y=j covers n+1-j points; diagonal line x+y=c covers points where a+b=c, with a,b≥1, a+b≤n+1, so c ranges from 2 to n+1. For each c, the number of points on diagonal x+y=c is c-1 (for c ≤ n+1). So diagonal lines can also cover many points.\n\nSunny lines can be any other slope, but they cannot be parallel to those three families. They can cover at most one point per column (since they are not vertical). Actually a sunny line can intersect each vertical line at most once, so at most one point per column. Similarly, at most one point per row, and at most one point per diagonal of slope -1? Not exactly, but they cannot be parallel to those families, so they intersect each vertical line at a unique point, each horizontal line at a unique point, each diagonal line at a unique point. So a sunny line can cover at most one point per column, per row, per diagonal. So a sunny line can cover at most n points? Actually there are n columns (x=1,...,n). So a sunny line can intersect each column at most once, so at most n points. But the points are only in the triangular region, so the maximum number of points a sunny line can cover is maybe n? But we need to consider the shape.\n\nBut the key is that a sunny line cannot cover two points in the same column, row, or diagonal. So for column n-2, we need a vertical line to cover all three points, because any sunny line can cover at most one of them. So we need at least one vertical line for column n-2. So we have at least one non-sunny line used for column n-2. Then we have n-2 non-sunny lines total (including that vertical line). So we have n-3 other non-sunny lines to allocate.\n\nNow consider column n-1: points (n-1,1) and (n-1,2). They are in same column, so a vertical line x=n-1 could cover both. But we might not have a vertical line for column n-1 if we want to use sunny lines for them. But a sunny line can cover at most one of them. So we need either a vertical line for column n-1, or two separate lines (one sunny, one non-sunny) covering each point. But we have only 2 sunny lines total. So we could allocate one sunny line to cover (n-1,1) and maybe also other points elsewhere, and another sunny line to cover (n-1,2) and maybe other points elsewhere. But we also need to cover (n,1). That is a third point in column n. So we have three points left: (n-1,1), (n-1,2), (n,1). As we argued earlier, any line connecting any two of these points is non-sunny. So a sunny line can cover at most one of them. So we need at least three lines to cover them. Since we have only 2 sunny lines left, we need at least one non-sunny line among them. But we have used one non-sunny line for column n-2. We have n-2 non-sunny lines total, so we have n-3 remaining non-sunny lines. So we could allocate a non-sunny line to cover some of these points. For example, we could use a vertical line x=n-1 to cover (n-1,1) and (n-1,2). That would be a non-sunny line. Then we would need to cover (n,1) with a sunny line. That would be okay: we have 2 sunny lines total, we could use one for (n,1) and maybe other points elsewhere. But we need to cover all other points in columns 1,...,n-2 (except those covered by vertical line x=n-2). We have n-2 non-sunny lines total, we used vertical line x=n-2 and vertical line x=n-1, that's 2 non-sunny lines. So we have n-4 non-sunny lines left to cover columns 1,...,n-3. There are n-3 columns left (x=1,...,n-3). We could use vertical lines for each of those columns, requiring n-3 lines. But we only have n-4 non-sunny lines left. So we cannot cover all those columns with vertical lines only. We could use some sunny lines to cover points in those columns. But we have only 2 sunny lines total, and we already used one sunny line for (n,1). The other sunny line could be used to cover some points in columns 1,...,n-3. But can a sunny line cover many points across multiple columns? Yes, a sunny line can intersect each column at most once, so it can cover at most one point per column. So a sunny line could cover at most n-3 points in columns 1,...,n-3 (one per column). But we need to cover all points in those columns. Let's see the total number of points in columns 1,...,n-3: column i has n+1-i points. So total points in columns 1,...,n-3 = sum_{i=1}^{n-3} (n+1-i) = sum_{t=4}^{n} t? Actually let's compute: sum_{i=1}^{n-3} (n+1-i) = sum_{j=4}^{n} j (where j = n+1-i). For i=1, j=n; i=n-3, j=4. So sum_{j=4}^{n} j = (sum_{j=1}^{n} j) - (1+2+3) = n(n+1)/2 - 6. So total points in columns 1,...,n-3 = n(n+1)/2 - 6. Meanwhile, we have n-4 vertical lines covering columns 1,...,n-4? Actually we have n-4 non-sunny lines left. We could allocate them as vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-4 (that's n-4 lines). Then column n-3 remains uncovered. Column n-3 has points (n-3,1), (n-3,2), (n-3,3), (n-3,4). That's 4 points. We have used vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-4 (n-4 lines). We have used vertical lines for columns n-2 and n-1 (2 lines). So total non-sunny lines used = (n-4) + 2 = n-2, which is all non-sunny lines. So we have no non-sunny lines left for column n-3. So we need to cover column n-3's 4 points with sunny lines. But we have only 2 sunny lines total, and we already used one for (n,1). So we have only one sunny line left to cover column n-3's 4 points. A sunny line can cover at most one point per column, so it can cover at most one point in column n-3. So we cannot cover all points in column n-3. So this arrangement fails.\n\nThus, the argument that k=2 is impossible seems plausible. However, we need to verify if there is any other arrangement that could work. Perhaps we could use diagonal lines (non-sunny) to cover multiple points across columns, reducing the need for vertical lines. For example, we could use diagonal lines x+y=c to cover points across columns. Let's explore more systematically.\n\nWe need to cover all points with n lines, exactly 2 sunny. So we have n-2 non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines can be vertical, horizontal, or diagonal (slope -1). Each non-sunny line can cover many points. The goal is to maximize coverage with non-sunny lines to reduce the need for sunny lines. Since we have only 2 sunny lines, we need to cover the rest with non-sunny lines.\n\nWe need to see if there is a known result: The answer is {0,1,3}. I recall a known problem about covering a triangular grid with lines, where the possible number of non-vertical/horizontal/diagonal lines is limited. The answer might indeed be {0,1,3}. Let's try to see if k=3 is possible. The solution says yes, construction exists using three sunny lines. For k≥4 impossible because sunny lines cover at most one point per column. That seems plausible: if we have k≥4 sunny lines, each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, so total points covered by sunny lines per column is at most k (since each sunny line can intersect each column at most once). But we have n columns, each column has at least one point (except maybe some columns have many points). Actually each column i has n+1-i points. So the total number of points that need to be covered by non-sunny lines is sum_{i=1}^{n} (n+1-i) - (max points covered by sunny lines). If we have k sunny lines, each can cover at most one point per column, so total points covered by sunny lines ≤ k * n (since each sunny line can intersect each column at most once). But we have n columns, each column has at least one point, so we need at least n points covered by non-sunny lines? Actually we need to cover all points. If we have k sunny lines, they can cover at most k points per column? Wait, each sunny line can intersect each column at most once, but there are n columns. So each sunny line can cover at most n points (one per column). So k sunny lines can cover at most k*n points. But total points = n(n+1)/2. So we need n-2 non-sunny lines to cover the rest. But the argument for k≥4 being impossible might be based on a more refined counting: each column i has n+1-i points. If we have k sunny lines, each column can have at most k points covered by sunny lines (since each sunny line can intersect column at most once). So the remaining points in column i that need to be covered by non-sunny lines is at least (n+1-i) - k. For columns where (n+1-i) > k, we need at least one non-sunny line covering that column. Actually a non-sunny line can cover many points in a column (vertical) or across columns (horizontal or diagonal). But we need to ensure we have enough non-sunny lines to cover all columns where the number of points exceeds k. Let's examine.\n\nIf k≥4, then for columns i where n+1-i > k, i.e., i n+1-k, we have columns with more than k points. For n≥3, if k≥4, then n+1-k ≤ n-3? Actually n+1-k ≤ n-3 if k≥4? Let's compute: n+1-k ≤ n-3 => 1-k ≤ -3 => k ≥ 4. So yes, for k≥4, columns i ≤ n-3 have more than k points? Let's check: column i = n-3 has n+1-(n-3) = 4 points. So if k≥4, column n-3 has exactly 4 points, which is ≤ k (if k=4). So columns with more than k points are those with i ≤ n-4 (since column n-4 has n+1-(n-4) = 5 points). So for k=4, columns i ≤ n-4 have at least 5 points > 4. So those columns need at least one non-sunny line covering them. So we need at least (n-4) non-sunny lines for those columns. But we have only n-2 non-sunny lines total. So we have enough? Actually n-2 non-sunny lines is more than n-4 for n≥3. So that doesn't give a contradiction. But the solution says k≥4 impossible because sunny lines cover at most one point per column. Let's examine more carefully.\n\nMaybe the argument is that each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, so total points covered by sunny lines ≤ k * n. But total points = n(n+1)/2. So we need n-2 non-sunny lines to cover the rest. But each non-sunny line can cover at most n points (vertical/horizontal/diagonal). So total coverage capacity of non-sunny lines is (n-2) * n = n^2 - 2n. Combined with sunny lines capacity k*n = kn. So total capacity = (k + n - 2) * n = (n + k - 2) * n. But we need to cover n(n+1)/2 points. So we need (n + k - 2) * n ≥ n(n+1)/2 => (n + k - 2) ≥ (n+1)/2 => k ≥ (n+1)/2 - n + 2 = (n+1 - 2n + 4)/2 = (5 - n)/2. For n≥3, RHS ≤ 1. So k≥something small. So that doesn't give a contradiction for k≥4.\n\nThus the argument for k≥4 being impossible must be more subtle. Let's think: The condition \"exactly k of the lines are sunny\" means we have exactly k lines that are not parallel to x-axis, y-axis, or x+y=0. So the other n-k lines are non-sunny. The non-sunny lines can be vertical, horizontal, or slope -1. So we have n-k non-sunny lines. The solution says for k≥4, impossible because sunny lines cover at most one point per column. But we have n-k non-sunny lines that can cover many points. So why is k≥4 impossible? Let's try to find a counterexample: maybe for n=5, k=4, we can have 4 sunny lines and 1 non-sunny line. Could we cover all points? Let's try to construct for n=5. Points are (a,b) with a,b≥1, a+b ≤ 6. So points: (1,1)-(1,5), (2,1)-(2,4), (3,1)-(3,3), (4,1)-(4,2), (5,1). That's 15 points. We have 5 lines total, exactly 4 sunny lines, 1 non-sunny line. The non-sunny line could be vertical x=1 covering points (1,1)-(1,5). That covers 5 points. The remaining 10 points need to be covered by 4 sunny lines. Each sunny line can intersect each column at most once, so each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. There are 5 columns. So each sunny line can cover at most 5 points. So 4 sunny lines can cover at most 20 points, which is enough. But we need to cover exactly the remaining points: (2,1)-(2,4), (3,1)-(3,3), (4,1)-(4,2), (5,1). That's 4+3+2+1 = 10 points. Could we assign 4 sunny lines to cover these 10 points? Possibly. Let's try to see if we can cover them with 4 lines such that each line is not vertical/horizontal/diagonal. For each column, we need to cover all points in that column. For column 2, we have points (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4). That's 4 points. A sunny line can cover at most one of them. So we need at least 4 lines to cover column 2's points. But we have only 4 sunny lines total, and we also need to cover points in other columns. So we cannot cover column 2's 4 points with only 4 sunny lines because we also need to cover points in columns 3,4,5. So we need at least 4 lines for column 2, plus additional lines for other columns. So k≥4 seems impossible because the column with the most points (column 2 for n=5) has n-1 points = 4. So we need at least n-1 lines to cover that column if we cannot use non-sunny lines for that column. But we have n-k non-sunny lines. For k≥4, n-k ≤ n-4. So we have at most n-4 non-sunny lines. For n=5, n-k ≤ 1. So we have at most 1 non-sunny line. That non-sunny line could be vertical covering column 2, covering all 4 points. Then we have 4 sunny lines left to cover the rest. That might be possible. Let's try: Use vertical line x=2 (non-sunny) covering (2,1)-(2,4). Then we have 4 sunny lines to cover columns 1,3,4,5. Column 1 has 5 points, column 3 has 3 points, column 4 has 2 points, column 5 has 1 point. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. So we need to cover 5+3+2+1 = 11 points with 4 sunny lines. Each sunny line can cover at most 4 points (since there are 4 columns left). Actually each sunny line can intersect each column at most once, so each sunny line can cover at most 4 points (one per column). So 4 sunny lines can cover at most 16 points, which is enough for 11 points. But we need to ensure we can cover all points with 4 lines. Let's try to assign points to lines: we need to cover each column's points. For column 1, we have 5 points. We need to assign each of those points to a different sunny line (since each line can cover at most one point in column 1). So we need at least 5 sunny lines to cover column 1's points if we cannot use non-sunny lines for column 1. But we have only 4 sunny lines. So we need a non-sunny line covering column 1 as well. But we only have one non-sunny line (vertical x=2). So we cannot cover column 1's 5 points. So k=4 fails for n=5.\n\nThus, the argument that k≥4 impossible is plausible: the column with the most points is column 1 (has n points). To cover column 1's n points, we need at least n lines that are either vertical (non-sunny) or each covering one point (sunny). Since we have only n-k non-sunny lines, we need at least n - (n-k) = k sunny lines to cover the remaining points in column 1. So we need at least k ≥ n to cover column 1's n points if we have no vertical line for column 1. But we could use a vertical line for column 1 (non-sunny). So we could allocate one non-sunny line to column 1, covering all n points. Then we have n-1 columns left, each with decreasing number of points. The column with the most points after column 1 is column 2 with n-1 points. To cover column 2's n-1 points, we need either a vertical line (non-sunny) or n-1 sunny lines. But we have only n-k-1 non-sunny lines left (since we used one for column 1). So we need to allocate enough sunny lines to cover column 2's points. If k≥4, we have at most n-4 sunny lines? Actually k≥4 means we have at most n-4 non-sunny lines. So we have at most n-4 non-sunny lines total. If we use one for column 1, we have at most n-5 non-sunny lines left. So we cannot allocate a vertical line for column 2 if n-5 1 (i.e., n ≤ 5). But for larger n, maybe we could allocate vertical lines for columns 1 and 2, leaving n-2 non-sunny lines used, leaving n-k-2 non-sunny lines left. But we need to cover all columns. Let's think more systematically.\n\nWe need to cover each column i (i from 1 to n) with points. For each column i, we can either use a vertical line (non-sunny) covering all points in that column, or we can cover each point in that column with distinct lines (sunny or other non-sunny lines that intersect column i at a single point). However, horizontal lines intersect each column at exactly one point (since they are horizontal). So a horizontal line can cover one point in each column. Similarly, diagonal lines also intersect each column at exactly one point. So any non-vertical non-sunny line (horizontal or diagonal) can cover at most one point per column. So the only way to cover multiple points in a column with a single line is to use a vertical line. So to reduce the number of lines needed, we need to use vertical lines for columns with many points.\n\nThus, the problem reduces to selecting a set of columns to be covered by vertical lines (non-sunny). Let V be the set of columns for which we use a vertical line. Then for each column i not in V, we need to cover its points using other lines (horizontal, diagonal, or sunny). Each of those lines can cover at most one point per column. So for each column i not in V, we need at least (n+1-i) lines (one per point) unless we can reuse the same line across columns (horizontal lines can cover one point per column, but they can cover points across columns). Actually a horizontal line y=j can cover one point in each column i where the point (i,j) exists (i.e., i ≤ n+1-j). So a horizontal line can cover multiple points across columns, but only one per column. Similarly, diagonal lines x+y=c can cover one point per column as well. Sunny lines also can cover one point per column. So we can use lines that intersect many columns, covering one point in each column. So the total number of lines needed to cover all points in columns not in V is at most the maximum number of points in any column not in V? Actually we need to cover all points across those columns, but we can use lines that intersect many columns. This is reminiscent of covering a bipartite grid with lines that intersect each column at most once. This is like a matching problem: we need to assign each point to a line such that each line can take at most one point per column. This is like a set of lines each being a function mapping column to row (or diagonal). So we need to cover all points with lines that are functions from columns to rows (or diagonal). This is similar to covering a set of points in a grid with \"transversals\". The minimal number of such lines need